const toleranceCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'nominal', label: 'nominal size', min: 5, max: 50, step: 1, value: 20, unit: 'mm' },
    { name: 'tol', label: 'tolerance (±)', min: 0.01, max: 0.5, step: 0.01, value: 0.05, unit: 'mm' },
    { name: 'measured', label: 'measured size', min: 4, max: 51, step: 0.01, value: 20.03, unit: 'mm' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'acceptable range', expr: 'nominal - tol', unit: 'to', },
    { label: 'upper limit', expr: 'nominal + tol', unit: 'mm' },
    { label: 'in tolerance?', expr: 'measured >= nominal - tol && measured <= nominal + tol', unit: '' },
  ],
};

const measureCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Metrology is the trade\'s grammar', note: 'Micrometers read to 0.01 mm, calipers to 0.02, gauge blocks are the reference truth. If you cannot measure it, you cannot make it.' },
    { math: '2 · Tolerances are the contract', note: 'A dimension with ±0.05 mm is a promise: every part inside the band is good, outside it is scrap. The tolerance sets the process.' },
    { math: '3 · Materials fight back differently', note: 'Aluminium cuts fast and galls; stainless work-hardens; cast iron machines dusty; titanium is strong, springy and hungry for sharp tools.' },
    { math: '4 · Heat is the hidden dimension', note: 'Metals expand with temperature — a part measured warm can be out of tolerance cold. Precision work controls temperature too.' },
    { math: '5 · Safety: sharp, spinning, hot', note: 'No gloves near rotating spindles, eye protection always, chips are razor-sharp and metal chips burn — the shop\'s rules are written in incidents.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/machining/foundational/measurement-and-materials">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Machining is the discipline of removing metal to a promise. A drawing that says
    20.00 ±0.05 mm is a contract: every part whose measured size lands between 19.95 and
    20.05 mm is good; anything outside is scrap, no matter how close it looks to the naked
    eye. The machinist's entire craft lives between those two ideas — making metal behave,
    then proving it did — which is why the foundational unit is not "how to run a lathe" but
    the two pillars underneath every machine: <strong>metrology</strong> (the science of
    measurement) and <strong>materials</strong> (how each alloy fights the cutting tool).
  </p>
  <p>
    The pairing is deliberate. You cannot hold a tolerance you cannot measure, and you cannot
    plan a cut you do not understand materially. A beginner who learns only machines produces
    parts that feel right and measure wrong; a machinist who knows the instruments and the
    metallurgy can walk up to an unfamiliar machine or unfamiliar alloy and still make a
    good part. Lathes, mills and CNC systems are powerful executors of decisions — the
    decisions happen here.
  </p>
  <p>
    The third thread is safety, and in machining it is genuinely foundational rather than
    bureaucratic: the shop contains machines that spin steel at thousands of revolutions per
    minute, throw chips hot enough to burn and sharp enough to suture with, and drench
    everything in coolant mist. The rules are short, absolute, and written by the injuries
    that preceded them — and they are tested in every machining credential, because the
    trade does not certify people who will hurt themselves in week two.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="measurement, tolerances, materials, heat, safety" widget="equationStep" config={measureCfg} />

  <h2>Precision measurement instruments</h2>
  <p>
    Metrology is the trade's grammar, learned in an ordered ladder of instruments. The
    <strong>steel rule</strong> starts it (readable to roughly 0.5 mm); the
    <strong>vernier caliper</strong> measures inside, outside, depth and step to about
    0.02 mm and is the daily workhorse; the <strong>micrometer</strong> — the screw, one of
    metrology's oldest and best ideas — reads to 0.01 mm with a vernier (and 0.001 mm for
    digital or fine versions), and is the instrument for anything called "precise". A
    25 mm outside micrometer has a fixed anvil and a spindle moved by a 40-threads-per-cm
    screw; one full spindle turn advances it exactly 0.5 mm, and the thimble's 50 divisions
    split that into 0.01 mm. Handling rules follow from the mechanism: clean the measuring
    faces, zero-check before use, use the friction thimble or ratchet so measurement force
    is consistent, and never over-torque — an anvil pressed hard measures less than the
    truth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Above the hand tools sits the reference hierarchy. <strong>Gauge blocks</strong> —
    hardened steel or ceramic blocks lapped so flat they wring together — are the shop's
    physical truth; combinations stack to any dimension, and calibrations trace them back to
    national standards (metrology's formal chain of traceability). The <strong>surface
    plate</strong> (granite, flat to microns) is the datum plane from which height gauges
    and scribes work; the <strong>dial indicator</strong> (0.01 mm per division) measures
    runout, alignment and deflection, and turns "is it straight?" into a number;
    <strong>plug, ring and thread gauges</strong> give go/no-go verdicts faster than any
    reading; and the <strong>coordinate measuring machine (CMM)</strong> probes features in
    three axes for the highest-accuracy inspection. The professional habits tie it all
    together: choose an instrument whose resolution is finer than a tenth of the tolerance,
    measure repeatedly and at temperature, and never trust a measurement whose instrument
    you have not verified.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="measured part vs its tolerance window" widget="sliderLab" config={toleranceCfg} />

  <h2>Tolerance: the promise you either keep or scrap</h2>
  <p>
    A dimension plus its tolerance defines an acceptance window, and the verdict is binary —
    the widget makes the arithmetic visible. But tolerance is also the economic engine of the
    trade: tighter tolerances cost disproportionately more, because error sources that vanish
    at ±0.2 mm dominate at ±0.02 mm. Machine tool accuracy, tool wear across a production
    run, fixturing, thermal growth, measurement uncertainty — every one must be a fraction
    of the tolerance band. This is why drawings assign tolerances by function (a clearance
    hole can be ±0.5 mm; a bearing journal needs ±0.01 mm), why process capability
    statistics (comparing process spread to the window) govern production machining, and
    why the first question about any part is "what tolerance?" — because the tolerance
    chooses the machine, the tooling, the sequence and the price.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> precision means always aiming for the exact nominal
    number. Machinists aim <em>inside</em> the window, deliberately offset toward the safe
    side — external dimensions slightly over nominal, holes slightly under — so that
    tool wear and finishing have room to work without crossing a limit. Targeting the
    middle of the band is engineering; targeting the nominal is hope.
  </p>

  <h2>Metals and their machinability</h2>
  <p>
    Machinability is not one number but a family of behaviours, and each major alloy family
    teaches a different lesson. <strong>Mild (low-carbon) steel</strong> is the reference:
    predictable, tough, forming good chips at moderate speeds. <strong>Cast iron</strong>
    machines fast with no coolant because its graphite makes chips crumble — at the cost of
    abrasive graphite dust everywhere. <strong>Aluminium</strong> shears beautifully at high
    speeds but is soft and sticky: it galls (welds itself to the tool), needs sharp
    geometry and often lubricant on threads, and its own family splits — free-machining
    alloys with lead or silicon versus sticky pure grades. <strong>Stainless steel</strong>
    (especially the common 304) work-hardens aggressively: a tool dwelling or rubbing
    instead of cutting hardens the surface under itself, so the next pass cuts even harder
    material — the machinist's countermeasures are constant feed, sharp tools and adequate
    depth. <strong>Titanium</strong> combines high strength, low elasticity (it springs away
    from the tool, rubbing instead of shearing) and terrible thermal conductivity: the
    cutting heat cannot leave in the chip, so it cooks the cutting edge — hence low speeds,
    flood coolant and rigid setups. <strong>Brass and bronze</strong> machine freely, which
    is why they are traditional apprentice metals.
  </p>
  <p>
    The physics underneath all of it is a triangle: <strong>speed</strong> (surface metres
    per minute), <strong>feed</strong> (advance per revolution or tooth) and <strong>depth
    of cut</strong>, interacting with tool material (high-speed steel versus carbide versus
    coated carbides) and coolant. Speed drives heat; feed and depth drive cutting force;
    and each material has a workable envelope. The shop skill is reading the cut — chip
    colour and shape, sound, surface finish — as feedback about where in the envelope you
    are: blue chips on steel mean speed (heat) is high; a squealing finish means rubbing,
    which means change feed or geometry before the tool dies.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a print: datums and the geometry behind the numbers</h2>
  <p>
    A machinist's drawing is a contract with two halves: dimensions and geometry. The
    dimensions are the sizes; the <strong>datums</strong> are the reference features the
    part is measured from — and knowing which surface is datum A, B and C decides how a
    part is held and how it is inspected, because measuring from the wrong surface can
    reject a good part or accept a bad one. Around the datums sits the language of
    <strong>geometric dimensioning and tolerancing (GD&amp;T)</strong>: symbols that control
    flatness (is this face planar within 0.05 mm?), perpendicularity, parallelism, position
    (where may this hole's true centre wander?) and concentricity. GD&amp;T looks arcane and
    is actually merciful: instead of stacking tolerances to force functions indirectly, it
    states the <em>functional requirement</em> directly — this bore must accept this shaft,
    this face must seal — and lets the machinist choose how to achieve it. The apprentice
    starts with the vocabulary (the feature control frame, the datum callouts) and the
    inspection habits that grow from it (check the datum surfaces first, then features
    against them); the full syntax comes with practice, but the idea — measure the part the
    way its function measures it — starts on the first print.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The shop's safety grammar</strong> is short and absolute because its hazards are
    mechanical and immediate. No gloves, loose sleeves, jewellery or long unrestrained hair
    near rotating machines — a spindle catching fabric pulls the whole body in faster than
    reaction time (the "no gloves" rule is counterintuitive to beginners and non-negotiable
    anyway). Safety glasses always, side shields around grinders; chips handled with hooks
    and brushes, never fingers (chips are razor-edged and hot enough to burn); machines
    stopped and spindle lowered to change work; compressed air used with discipline (it
    launches chips into eyes); coolant mist and metal dust managed by ventilation. Machine
    guarding, E-stops and lockout on maintenance join the general shop canon. The rules are
    tested on every machining credential, and they are the reason the trade's injury rates
    can be low while its hazards are fearsome.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Heat is the hidden dimension</strong> in both measurement and materials. Metals
    expand roughly linearly with temperature — steel about 11.7 µm per metre per °C — so a
    100 mm steel part measured 10 °C above the 20 °C reference temperature has grown about
    12 µm, which is a large fraction of a ±0.02 mm tolerance. Precision shops therefore
    control temperature (20 °C metrology rooms, soak time so parts and gauges reach the same
    temperature) and, at the cutting edge, manage heat directly: coolant delivery, chip
    evacuation (the chip carries most cutting heat away), and tool coatings that resist the
    temperatures that soften edges. The micrometer itself grows with your hand — one more
    reason measurement is a procedure, not a gesture.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Metrology and materials converge in the professional's judgement.</strong> A
    worn micrometer lies confidently; a mis-chosen cutting speed shortens tool life
    exponentially (Taylor's classic tool-life relation links speed to life); a work-hardened
    stainless surface from yesterday's dwell ruins today's insert. Machinists who advance —
    toward CNC programming, tooling engineering, quality and inspection — are the ones who
    kept both pillars: the humility to measure everything, and the metallurgy to predict
    what the metal will do before the machine is switched on. Everything else the trade
    does is built on that pair.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A speeds-and-feeds worked example grounds the materials section.</strong> Take a
    12 mm carbide end mill cutting 6061 aluminium: cutting-data tables suggest a surface
    speed around 250 m/min, which converts to spindle speed n = (1000 × V) ÷ (π × D) ≈
    (1000 × 250) ÷ (3.14 × 12) ≈ 6,600 rpm; the feed table suggests 0.1 mm per tooth, and
    with four flutes the table feed is n × flutes × per-tooth ≈ 6,600 × 4 × 0.1 ≈ 2,600
    mm/min. Now swap the alloy to 304 stainless: surface speed drops to around 40 m/min,
    so the same cutter runs near 1,000 rpm with a heavier per-tooth feed (to stay under the
    work-hardened surface) and flood coolant. Same machine, same cutter, tenfold speed
    difference — the metallurgy of the section above, converted into two numbers every
    machinist calculates before the spindle starts. The calculation itself (surface speed
    to rpm, per-tooth feed to table feed) is basic arithmetic; knowing the inputs — which
    surface speed an alloy and tool can tolerate — is the materials knowledge, and the
    two are inseparable in practice.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NIMS Machining credential standards; Hoffman &amp; Hopewell, <em>Precision Machining Technology</em>; Machinery's Handbook.</p>
</TopicPage>
